Git Branching Strategy and Workflow for DevOps

Basic Git Concepts
Git is a distributed version control system used to track changes in source code. It allows multiple developers to work on the same codebase simultaneously, maintain history of changes, 
and merge updates seamlessly. Branching is one of the most essential features in Git, enabling parallel work streams without affecting the main codebase.

Version Control System (VCS)
A version control system tracks changes in files over time. A distributed VCS like Git gives every developer a full copy of the repository history, enabling local commits, branching, and offline work.

Git Workflow Stages
Git workflow typically involves:
Working directory where changes are made
Staging area where changes are added before commit
Local repository where commits are stored
Remote repository where code is shared with others

Initializing a Git Repository
git init

Status of Files
git status

Staging Changes
git add filename
git add .

Committing Changes
git commit -m "Commit message"

Basic Git Commands
git log
git diff
git reset --hard HEAD~1
git remote add origin https://github.com/username/repo.git
git push -u origin main
git pull origin main
git clone https://github.com/username/repo.git

Branching in Git
A branch in Git is a pointer to a specific line of development. Branches allow isolated work on features, fixes, and experiments without disturbing the main branch.

Create a Branch
git branch branch-name

View Branches
git branch

Switch to a Branch
git checkout branch-name

Create and Switch
git checkout -b branch-name

Git Branching Strategies
Main Branch
The main branch (often named main or master) contains stable code. All finished and tested work is merged here.

Feature Branch
Develop individual features on separate branches. Naming convention often uses feature/<feature-name>. Example:
git checkout -b feature/login-functionality

Bugfix Branch
For bug fixes, create a branch named bugfix/<issue-name>. Example:
git checkout -b bugfix/login-error

Release Branch
A release branch is created when preparing for a new release version. It allows final testing and bug fixing before merging into main.

Hotfix Branch
Hotfix branches are used to quickly patch production issues. They are typically branched off the main branch and merged back after the fix.

Branching Best Practices
Create branches for every new feature or fix
Name branches clearly using prefixes feature/, bugfix/, hotfix/, release/
Commit frequently with meaningful messages
Keep branches up to date by merging main into feature branches periodically

Merging Branches
Once work in a branch is complete and tested, merge back into the target branch (often main or develop). Command:
git merge branch-name

Fast-Forward and No-Fast-Forward
Merges can be fast-forward when there are no divergent changes, or create a merge commit when there are parallel changes.

Git Rebase
Rebasing reapplies commits from one branch onto another. It can keep history cleaner but should be used carefully in shared branches. Example:
git rebase main

GitHub Collaboration
GitHub allows developers to push branches to remote repositories, open pull requests, review code, and merge changes.

Push Branch to GitHub
git push origin branch-name

Create Pull Request
Use GitHub UI to open a pull request from the branch to the target branch, then review and merge.

Resolving Merge Conflicts
When the same section of code is modified in different branches, Git reports a conflict. Open each conflicting file, make corrections, then:
git add filename
git commit

Branching Workflow Patterns
Feature Branch Workflow
Developers create a branch for each feature and merge back via pull requests after review.

Gitflow Workflow
Gitflow is a popular branching model with well-defined branch types such as feature, release, hotfix, and support branches.

Forking Workflow
Fork a repository into your own namespace, create branches for work, then submit pull requests to the original repository.

Step-by-Step Example
Initialize repository:
git init
Create main branch and push:
git remote add origin https://github.com/username/repo.git
git push -u origin main
Create feature branch:
git checkout -b feature/new-ui
Make changes, stage, commit:
git add .
git commit -m "Add new UI pages"
Push branch:
git push origin feature/new-ui
Open pull request and merge into main

Conclusion
Understanding branching strategies and workflows is critical for collaborating effectively in teams, maintaining a clean code history, and managing releases. Feature branches, bugfix branches,
hotfix branches, merging, rebasing, and pull requests are key skills required in modern DevOps source control practices.
